@page "/docs/components/icons"
@using Blaizio.Docs.Examples.Icons
<PageTitle>Icons - Blaizio</PageTitle>

<div class="typeset typeset-docs space-y-10">
    <header class="docs-page-header space-y-2.5">
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
            <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Icons</h1>
            <DocBadges Slug="icons" />
        </div>
        <p>Five icon sets - Tabler, Lucide, Phosphor, Remix Icon and Hugeicons - rendered by one component, <code>BzIcon</code>. Every member (<code>Tabler.Outline.Home</code>, <code>Lucide.Outline.House</code>, <code>Phosphor.Bold.House</code>...) is a typed <code>Icon</code> value that carries its own paint, grid and stroke width, so a single value is all you pass and the sets mix freely. Colour follows <code>currentColor</code>; size it with a CSS class.</p>
    </header>

    <UsageBlock Example="typeof(IconsUsageExample)"
                Description="Pass a typed Icon - no string, no family flag. Every set is one package away; the CLI installs Tabler, the set the components use." />

    <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Browse</h2>
        <p>Every icon of every set, live. Pick the set and the family, filter by name, and click an icon to copy the member you pass to <code>BzIcon</code>.</p>
        <IconBrowser />
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Sets and packages</h2>
        <p><code>Blaizio.Icons</code> carries <code>BzIcon</code> and the <code>Icon</code> type; every set is a package on top of it, so an app only ships the sets it uses. The <code>blaizio</code> CLI installs <code>Blaizio.Icons.Tabler</code> with every project - the styled components draw from it. They are versioned together with <code>Blaizio.Icons</code>, and <code>blaizio update</code> moves the ones your project references in lockstep.</p>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr><th>Set</th><th>Package</th><th>Members</th><th>Licence</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><a href="https://tabler.io/icons" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tabler Icons</a></td><td><code>Blaizio.Icons.Tabler</code></td><td><code>Tabler.Outline.*</code>, <code>Tabler.Filled.*</code></td><td>MIT</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="https://lucide.dev" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lucide</a></td><td><code>Blaizio.Icons.Lucide</code></td><td><code>Lucide.Outline.*</code></td><td>ISC</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="https://phosphoricons.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Phosphor Icons</a></td><td><code>Blaizio.Icons.Phosphor</code></td><td><code>Phosphor.Thin.*</code>, <code>Light</code>, <code>Regular</code>, <code>Bold</code>, <code>Fill</code>, <code>Duotone</code></td><td>MIT</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="https://remixicon.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Remix Icon</a></td><td><code>Blaizio.Icons.Remix</code></td><td><code>Remix.Line.*</code>, <code>Remix.Fill.*</code></td><td>Apache-2.0</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="https://hugeicons.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hugeicons</a> (free set)</td><td><code>Blaizio.Icons.HugeIcons</code></td><td><code>HugeIcons.StrokeRounded.*</code></td><td>MIT</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p>Add a set with the package manager or the CLI:</p>
        <CodeBlock Code="dotnet add package Blaizio.Icons.Lucide" Language="bash" />
        <p>The icon data keeps each set's own licence (shipped as <code>THIRD-PARTY-LICENSE.txt</code> in the package); the package code is MIT like the rest of Blaizio.</p>
    </section>

    <ExamplesHeading />

    <Demo Title="Basic" Description="Pass an Icon from Tabler.Outline or Tabler.Filled - no string, no family flag."
          Example="typeof(IconsBasicExample)" />

    <Demo Title="Outline and filled" Description="Tabler's two families; the Icon carries which it is."
          Example="typeof(IconsFamiliesExample)" />

    <Demo Title="Five sets, one component" Description="The same house from each set. Every Icon knows its grid and stroke width - Phosphor's 256 grid, Hugeicons' 1.5 stroke - so BzIcon renders all of them at their true weight with no parameter."
          Example="typeof(IconsSetsExample)" />

    <Demo Title="Weights" Description="Phosphor's six weights are six families: the weight is part of the member, not a parameter."
          Example="typeof(IconsWeightsExample)" />

    <Demo Title="Sizing" Description="Set the size with a class (e.g. Tailwind size-*); the stroke scales with it."
          Example="typeof(IconsSizingExample)" />

    <Demo Title="Customization" Description="Colour follows currentColor, so tint an icon with any text-* utility - or drop it into a coloured chip. Size and colour are pure classes."
          Example="typeof(IconsCustomExample)" />

    <Demo Title="With tooltip" Description="Wrap an icon in a Tooltip for an accessible hint - the icon (in a span, so it can receive focus and hover) is the trigger via RenderAs."
          Example="typeof(IconsTooltipExample)" />

    <Demo Title="RTL" Description="Wrapped in a DirectionProvider - the icon row mirrors and flows from the right."
          Example="typeof(IconsRtlExample)" />

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Bundle size: you only ship what you use</h2>
        <p>Each package carries a whole set - Tabler about 6,200 icons, Lucide 1,800, Phosphor 9,000 across its weights, Remix 3,200, Hugeicons 6,000 - and a real app references a few dozen. Only those ship.</p>
        <BzAlert>
            <BzIcon Icon="@Tabler.Outline.InfoCircle" />
            <BzAlertTitle>Trimming keeps only the icons you reference</BzAlertTitle>
            <BzAlertDescription>
                <p>Every icon package is trimmable: each icon is a self-contained property with no whole-set constructor, so a Blazor WebAssembly Release publish with <code>PublishTrimmed</code> (on by default) drops every icon the app never references. On this site that takes <code>Blaizio.Icons.Tabler</code> from about 3.2 MB to about 5 KB over the wire. The browser above loads its icons from JSON on demand for the same reason.</p>
                <p>Two things defeat it: turning <code>PublishTrimmed</code> off, and reaching icons through reflection instead of a direct member such as <code>Tabler.Outline.Home</code>.</p>
                <p>Measure the real payload in <code>publish/wwwroot/_framework</code> after a Release publish. The copy under <code>bin/</code> that <code>build</code> and <code>dotnet run</code> serve is untrimmed. On Blazor Server the assembly loads on the host and nothing is downloaded.</p>
            </BzAlertDescription>
        </BzAlert>
    </section>
</div>
